          "text": "The search didn't give a direct doc. Let me try to find the specific MSDN/StackOverflow discussion about this behavior. Let me try a more targeted search on Stack Overflow via Bing, or try fetching a known SO question.\n\nLet me search for \"import library not regenerated\" or \"link /DLL lib not updated\".\n\nActually, let me think about the mechanism more carefully, because I want to give an accurate answer.\n\nThe empirical evidence:\n1. `link /DEBUG /DLL /OUT:common_c.dll objs...` runs → common_c.dll, common_c.exp, common_c.pdb refreshed, common_c.lib NOT refreshed.\n2. This happened in both the working folder and the clone.\n\nThe reason: MSVC's link.exe, when producing a DLL, generates an import library. But it doesn't blindly rewrite it. The linker tracks whether the export list changed. If the export list is unchanged, the import library content would be identical, so the linker leaves the existing .lib alone.\n\nActually — I want to reconsider. Is it really \"exports unchanged\"? Or is it something about the linker's behavior with existing files and timestamps?\n\nLet me think about how the linker actually works. The linker's import library generation: it uses `lib.exe` internally (the librarian) to create the import lib from the .exp file. Actually, link.exe generates the .exp and then creates the .lib. \n\nHmm, there's a known detail: when the linker creates a DLL, it calls the librarian to build the import library. And there's a documented behavior in `LIB` about \"up-to-date\" import libraries. \n\nActually, I recall there's a specific documented note. Let me search the Microsoft docs for the /IMPLIB option page, which might mention this. URL: https://learn.microsoft.com/cpp/build/reference/implib-name-import-library\n\nLet me fetch that page. Also the /DLL page: https://learn.microsoft.com/cpp/build/reference/dll-build-a-dll\n\nLet me fetch both.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. Is there possibly a DIFFERENT explanation that doesn't require \"linker skips lib\"?\n\nAlternative: What if the .lib is created by `lib.exe` (the librarian), which is invoked by `link.exe`, and `lib.exe` has an \"up-to-date check\" where it compares the new .exp against the existing .lib and skips rewriting if the exports are the same? \n\nActually, here's a cleaner hypothesis based on how MSVC link works: The linker determines the exports, generates the .exp file (which always gets written since it's the current build's export record), then creates the import library from the .exp using the librarian. The librarian `lib.exe` when creating an import library checks if the library is already up-to-date (the .lib has a matching \"export\" record) and skips the rewrite. \n\nHmm, I'm not sure about the exact internal mechanism. Let me look for documentation. Let me fetch the MS Learn pages.\n\nLet me fetch:\n1. https://learn.microsoft.com/cpp/build/reference/implib-name-import-library\n2. https://learn.microsoft.com/cpp/build/reference/dll-build-a-dll\n\nThese are docs pages. Let me get them.",
