          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The documentation confirms:\n- `/DLL` builds a DLL. Exports via `__declspec(dllexport)` etc.\n- `/IMPLIB` — LINK creates the import library when building a program with exports. \"The default name is formed from the base name of the main output file and the extension `.lib`.\"\n- Important note: \"LINK ignores /IMPLIB when an import library isn't being created. If no exports are specified, LINK doesn't create an import library. **If an export file is used in the build, LINK assumes that an import library already exists and doesn't create one.**\"\n\nInteresting. That last sentence: \"If an export file is used in the build, LINK assumes that an import library already exists and doesn't create one.\"\n\nHmm, but that's about when you provide an .exp file as input. Not our case.\n\nThe docs don't explicitly state the \"skip rewriting when exports unchanged\" behavior. So my understanding of the exact mechanism needs to be framed carefully.\n\nLet me reconsider the actual mechanism more carefully. Let me think about what really happens in our case:\n\nThe DLL exports symbols via `__declspec(dllexport)` (COMMON_EXPORTS is defined, so geometry.hpp/string_utils.hpp mark functions with __declspec(dllexport)). \n\nWhen link runs: \n1. It builds the DLL.\n2. It generates the import library.\n\nBut empirically the .lib is not rewritten when exports unchanged. Why?\n\nLet me reconsider — maybe the mechanism is different. Maybe the linker DOES try to write the .lib, but... Actually, let me reconsider whether the linker uses `lib.exe` under the hood and whether lib.exe has an up-to-date check.\n\nActually, I now strongly recall the real mechanism: **link.exe delegates import-library creation to `lib.exe`, and `lib.exe` skips rewriting the library if its contents would be identical to the existing file.** Specifically, LIB has an \"up-to-date check\" — actually LIB always rebuilds... hmm.\n\nLet me look at the LIB reference page. Let me fetch https://learn.microsoft.com/cpp/build/reference/lib-reference and the LIB \"up-to-date\" related docs.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider from a completely different angle. Let me reconsider whether the .lib is actually \"not regenerated because exports unchanged\" vs. some other mechanism entirely.\n\nLet me look at the actual evidence once more:\n- Working folder: common_c.lib = 21.08 22:09:30. The C dll was relinked at 8:47 (objs 8:47) and 10:55. lib not touched either time.\n- Clone: common_cpp.lib = 10:33:15, relinked at 10:50:55. lib not touched.\n\nNow here's an important consideration: WAS the export table actually unchanged between those links? Yes — no export changes (same functions). So \"exports unchanged → skip\" is consistent.\n\nBut is there another explanation? What if the linker checks the mtime of the existing .lib against the .exp file it just generated, and if .lib is newer than... no, .lib (21.08) is much older than .exp (10:55) yet wasn't rewritten. So not a \"newer than\" check.\n\nWhat if the linker compares the exports defined in the current build against the exports recorded in the existing .lib, and only rewrites if they differ? That's the \"exports unchanged → skip\" hypothesis. It fits.\n\nLet me try to find a definitive source. Let me look at the LIB reference or search SO. Let me fetch the LIB reference page and maybe a targeted SO question via Bing with different query.\n\nActually, let me search SO directly with a site query via Bing: \"link.exe does not regenerate import library site:stackoverflow.com\". Let me try.\n\nActually, a better approach: let me recall the actual well-known behavior. I'm now quite confident this is a documented MSVC linker behavior. The linker maintains import libraries and does NOT regenerate them if the exports are unchanged. This is exactly why in the MSBuild/VS world, the import library is sometimes listed as a separate target and can cause \"LNK1104\" if stale or locked.\n\nHmm, but I want to give the user a solid, honest answer with appropriate confidence levels. The key points:\n\n1. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] The .lib is not rewritten when the DLL is relinked with unchanged exports — proven by their own disk evidence (both folders).\n\n2. The mechanism: The import library is a *derived artifact* — its content is fully determined by the DLL's export table, not by the DLL's code. When the exports are unchanged, the linker knows the resulting .lib would be byte-identical to the existing one, so it deliberately skips rewriting it (saves I/O). The .exp is an intermediate of the current link run and always gets regenerated.\n\n3. Why the DLL binary itself IS rewritten: the DLL contains the code (functions), which changed.\n\nThis is the honest explanation. I should mark confidence: the BEHAVIOR is fact; the internal \"why\" (deliberate optimization) is my understanding of the rationale — reasonably confident but let me try to find a source.\n\nLet me try one more targeted fetch: search SO for \"import library not regenerated\". Let me use Bing with a different query.\n\nActually, let me try to fetch the LIB reference page, which the /IMPLIB page references: https://learn.microsoft.com/cpp/build/reference/lib-reference\n\nAnd also let me try a StackOverflow question. Let me search via Bing for \"link /DLL lib not updated timestamps\". Actually the earlier Bing search returned generic results. Let me try DuckDuckGo html search which is more fetchable: https://html.duckduckgo.com/html/?q=...\n\nLet me try DuckDuckGo.",
