          "text": "The user asks: \"в makefile существует типа $^ но 'все пререквизиты кроме последнего'?\"\n\nThey want to know if there's a make automatic variable that means \"all prerequisites except the last one\".\n\nThe GNU make automatic variables are:\n- `$@` — target name\n- `$<` — first prerequisite\n- `$^` — all prerequisites\n- `$+` — all prerequisites (with duplicates)\n- `$?` — prerequisites newer than target\n- `$*` — stem\n- `$|` — order-only prerequisites\n- `$(@D)`, `$(@F)` — dir/file of target\n- `$(*D)`, `$(*F)` — dir/file of stem\n- `$(<D)`, `$(<F)` — dir/file of first prerequisite\n- `$^D`, `$^F` — dirs/files of all prerequisites\n\nThere's no built-in automatic variable for \"all prerequisites except the last\". But you can construct it using functions, e.g., `$(wordlist 1, $(words $^)...)` or `$(filter-out ...)`. \n\nThe common idiom for \"all but the last\": \n- Number of words: `$(words $^)`\n- All but last: `$(wordlist 1, $(words $^), ...)` — wait, that doesn't work directly. To get all but the last word of a list `$^`:\n  ```\n  $(wordlist 1, $(words $^), $^)  # this is ALL words\n  ```\n  Actually to drop the last word:\n  ```\n  $(wordlist 1, $(subst $() $(),...)...)\n  ```\n  The standard trick: \n  ```\n  $(wordlist 1, $(words $(filter-out $(lastword $^), $^)), $^)\n  ```\n  Hmm, that's complicated. Simpler:\n  ```\n  $(wordlist 1, $(words $^), $^)\n  ```\n  gives all words. To get all but last:\n  ```\n  $(wordlist 1, $(words $^)-1, ...)\n  ```\n  You can't do arithmetic inline easily in GNU make (no arithmetic on integers except $(words) and string ops). \n\nThe typical idiom to remove the last word:\n  ```\n  $(filter-out $(lastword $^), $^)\n  ```\n  But that removes ALL occurrences of the last word, not just the last. Not robust if duplicates.\n\nActually the cleanest standard idiom for \"all but last\":\n  ```\n  $(wordlist 1, $(words $^), $^) \n  ```\n  no.\n\nHmm. Let me think. GNU make doesn't have arithmetic, but there's a trick using `$(wordlist 1, $(words ...))`. To drop the last element, people use:\n  ```\n  $(wordlist 1, $(words $(filter-out $(lastword $^), $^)), ...)\n  ```\n  messy.\n\nActually, there is a well-known idiom using `$(eval)` and a computed count, or simply:\n  ```\n  ALL_BUT_LAST = $(wordlist 1, $(words $(subst ...,...) ))\n  ```\n\nActually the simplest and most common approach: since you can't subtract, people often restructure so the \"last prerequisite\" is a separate variable, and then use `$^` minus that last one via `$(filter-out $(LAST), $^)` when the last is unique.\n\nBut wait — the user's context: they were discussing the fix for the dll relink problem. In the grouped target recipe, `$^` = all the dll objs (CPP_DLL_OBJ). Maybe they're thinking about a fix where they want \"all prerequisites except the last one\" for something. Or maybe they're just curious about make features in general.\n\nActually, this seems like a knowledge question about make. Let me answer it directly.\n\nThe answer:\n- Built-in automatic variable for \"all but last\" does NOT exist.\n- `$^` = all prerequisites (deduplicated), `$+` = all with duplicates, `$<` = first, `$?` = newer-than-target ones.\n- To get \"all but last\", you use make functions. The standard idiom:\n  ```\n  $(wordlist 1, $(words $^), $^)\n  ```\n  gives all; to get all but last:\n  ```\n  $(wordlist 1, $(words $^), $^)   # no\n  ```\n  \n  Real idiom to drop the last word:\n  ```\n  $(wordlist 1, $(words $(filter-out $(lastword $^), $^)), $^)\n  ```\n  Wait, `$(words $(filter-out $(lastword $^), $^))` gives the count of the list minus all occurrences of the last word. If last word appears only once, that's N-1. Then `$(wordlist 1, N-1, $^)` gives first N-1 words. So:\n  ```\n  $(wordlist 1, $(words $(filter-out $(lastword $^), $^)), $^)\n  ```\n  This works IF the last word is unique. If it appears multiple times, filter-out removes all and count shrinks incorrectly (wordlist would then drop too many). Actually if lastword appears twice, words of filter-out = N-2, and wordlist 1..N-2 would give N-2 words = drop the last TWO. So not robust.\n\n  A more robust approach uses `$(subst)` with a unique separator, but GNU make subst is text-based.\n\nHonestly, for the user's context (the dll recipe), the cleanest idiom is `$(filter-out $(LAST), $^)` where LAST is a separate variable — because in their case, do they even need \"all but last\"? Let me reconsider why they're asking.\n\nContext: The fix proposal was to delete the old .lib before linking. The recipe currently:\n```\n$(CPP_SHARED) $(CPP_SHARED_LINK) &: $(CPP_DLL_OBJ) | $(BINDIR)/common\n\t$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(DLL) $(LINK_OUT)$(CPP_SHARED) $^\n```\n\nHmm, maybe the user is thinking of a different approach for the fix — like the \"touch\" idea, or maybe they want to pass all prerequisites to the link except the last one? Or maybe they're thinking about how to reference \"all objs except main.obj\"? \n\nActually — wait. Maybe they're exploring a fix idea like: \"I want the .lib to be the last prerequisite and use 'all except last' in the recipe\". For example, they might want:\n```\ncommon_c.dll common_c.lib &: geometry_c_dll.obj string_utils_c_dll.obj\n\t$(LINK) ... $(filter-out ...) \n```\n\nHmm, but I shouldn't over-speculate. The user asked a direct factual question: \"does make have something like $^ but 'all prerequisites except the last one'?\"\n\nAnswer: No built-in automatic variable. But it can be computed with functions. Give the idiom.\n\nLet me be precise and accurate about GNU make automatic variables:\n- `$@` — имя цели\n- `$<` — первый пререквизит\n- `$^` — все пререквизиты, без дубликатов, в порядке объявления\n- `$+` — все пререквизиты С дубликатами\n- `$?` — пререквизиты, которые новее цели\n- `$|` — order-only пререквизиты\n- `$*` — stem (основа, из которой выросла цель)\n- modifiers `D`/`F` for dirname/basename: `$(@D)`, `$(@F)`, `$(<D)`, `$^D`, `$+F` etc.\n\nNone = \"all but last\". \n\nIdioms to get \"all but last\":\n1. `$(filter-out $(lastword $^), $^)` — removes all words equal to the last one. Works if the last word is unique in the list (common case for their objs list).\n2. More general, positional: \n   ```\n   $(wordlist 1, $(words $^), $^)  # all\n   ```\n   To get \"all except last\" robustly regardless of duplicates, you'd need a count-minus-one. GNU make can't subtract numbers natively, but there are tricks. The common robust idiom:\n   ```\n   define drop_last\n   $(wordlist 1, $(words $(filter-out $(lastword $1), $1)), $1)\n   endef\n   ```\n   still has the duplicate issue.\n\n   Actually there IS a way to get N-1 using $(wordlist) with a computed argument... no arithmetic. People use `$(eval n := $(words $^))` then... still can't subtract.\n\n   The genuinely robust way without arithmetic: reverse, drop first, reverse. There's a `$(reverse)`? GNU make 4.4 doesn't have reverse... Actually GNU make 4.4 does NOT have a built-in reverse (there's no $(reverse) in make). People write recursive macros for reverse. Overkill.\n\nGiven the practical context, the simplest and most readable: `$(filter-out $(lastword $^), $^)` when the last prerequisite is a distinct file (e.g., a specific .lib or a specific object) that won't appear elsewhere in the list. And actually even better for their case: name the \"last\" explicitly.\n\nBut wait — I should reconsider: is the user maybe confusing something? They might be under the impression that `$^` is \"all prerequisites\", and they want \"all except the last\". Maybe in their recipe the LAST prerequisite is special (e.g., the order-only dir? no, order-only is separate `$|`).\n\nHmm, actually, maybe their thought: for the dll fix, they want to link with \"all prerequisites except the import lib\"? Like the recipe could reference objs and lib separately.\n\nI should answer the factual question, give the idiom, and note the caveat about duplicates. Keep it concise. Also mention `$+` (with duplicates) and `$?`