tually does. It's the kind of thing that bites later when something more complex is layered on (CMake, parallel builds, CI). In a parallel `make -j`, the perpetual relink could create races (e.g., link.exe rewriting the dll while a test process has it loaded → \"access denied\"). Actually — the journal already mentions a past \"отказано в доступе\" issue. The perpetual relink increases the chance of file-lock collisions.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the severity more carefully:\n\n1. **Every run**: `make test` relinks both dlls. Adds maybe 1-2 seconds. Annoying but tolerable.\n\n2. **Danger of the \".lib appears fresh to make but isn't\"** — actually the reverse: make thinks it's fresh after link, but it's not. So make won't rebuild t3/t4 exes based on .lib changes. But since .lib only changes when exports change, and when exports change the .lib IS rewritten (fresh timestamp), make WILL rebuild the exes then. So the only divergence is: make thinks lib is fresh when it isn't (but content is actually unchanged and correct). Harmless for correctness.\n\n3. **The actual hazard in production-style builds**: If a consumer exe is linked and the .lib is genuinely stale (exports DID change but the old .lib somehow survives)... can that happen? If exports change, link regenerates the lib. So no.\n\nWait — actually there's a REAL hazard I should think about. Let me reconsider the scenario where exports change:\n\nScenario: user adds a new exported function to geometry.hpp. Then:\n- geometry_cpp_dll.obj recompiles.\n- common_cpp.dll group: obj newer → relink → exports changed → linker REGENERATES common_cpp.lib (fresh timestamp).\n- t3/t4 exes depend on the .lib → make sees .lib fresh → relinks exes. \n\nGood — that works correctly. So the mechanism actually self-heals when it matters (exports change).\n\nSo conclusion: **Not critical for correctness. It's a hygiene/performance issue.** The perpetual relink wastes a bit of time every run but the resulting artifacts are always correct. It becomes more than cosmetic when: (a) parallel make (races on locked dll), (b) it hides real staleness signals (make can't distinguish \"relinked for real\" from \"relinked because lib looks old\"), (c) on CI it wastes minutes over many runs, (d) it taught you to distrust make output — a real cost for a learning project.\n\nActually, I want to also address: is there any scenario where this could produce an INCORRECT binary? Let me think hard.\n\nThe danger would be: t3/t4 exe links against an OUTDATED .lib while the DLL has new exports. When would that happen? If the .lib was NOT regenerated while the DLL's exports changed. But we established the linker regenerates the lib when exports change. So the lib and dll are always consistent with each other's exports (both from the same link run, or lib older-but-identical content). \n\nThe consumer exe links against the lib → the import descriptors (DLL name + function names) get baked into the exe's import table. At runtime, the loader resolves against the DLL on disk by name. As long as lib's export list matches dll's actual exports, runtime resolution works. Since lib content = old export list = same as current dll exports (unchanged), it's consistent. When exports change, lib regenerates. So consistency holds.\n\nOne more edge: what if the user REMOVES an exported function? Then lib regenerates (exports changed). Consumers that still call the removed function → link error at exe link time. Fine, caught.\n\nSo genuinely not critical for correctness.\n\n**Assessment recommendation**: \n- If this were the student's own project ending now (1st stage), it's tolerable — tests pass, artifacts correct. \n- But it's cheap to fix and worth fixing before it bites: the fix (delete .lib before link) is 2 lines. And there's a learning value: understanding the make/link divergence. \n- It becomes actually painful on CI/parallel builds.\n\nGiven the anti-sycophancy rules, I should be honest: it's NOT critical (correctness