          "text": "The user asks three conceptual questions about the -MMD -MP mechanism. Let me answer each precisely and pedagogically (this is a learning context).\n\n**Q1: `.d` файлы - список заголовков которые включает эта программа?**\n\nYes, essentially. The `.d` file is a make-format rule that lists, for a given object file, all the source files and headers it depends on — i.e., the transitive closure of `#include`s (for the compilation unit), excluding system headers (because `-MMD` uses `-MM` semantics which skips system headers like `<vector>`, `<string>`).\n\nAn example .d file for t1 main.cpp:\n```\nbuild/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.o: t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp common/geometry.hpp\n```\nIt's literally a make rule in the same syntax as a Makefile: `target: prereqs`. So make can read it directly.\n\nKey point: it's not just the headers YOU directly include — it's the transitive closure (headers included by headers). And it's generated by the preprocessor scanning `#include` lines. So if geometry.hpp includes something else, that gets listed too.\n\nActually let me be careful: the .d lists all files that the preprocessor opened via #include (both direct and transitive), minus system headers (with -MM). So yes — \"список всего, что этот .o собрался из / зависит от\".\n\n**Q2: что значит -MMD выполняет компиляцию? компилирует .d файлы или что?**\n\nThe user is confused by my statement \"при компиляции пишет зависимости в .d, компиляцию при этом выполняет (в отличие от -M/-MM, которые только генерируют и не компилируют)\".\n\nExplanation: The compiler does TWO jobs when given `-MMD`:\n1. Compiles the .cpp → produces the .o (the actual compilation, the main job).\n2. As a side effect, during preprocessing, it scans the #include lines and writes the .d file.\n\nThe distinction between flag families:\n- `-M` / `-MM`: tells the compiler to ONLY output dependencies and NOT do the compilation. The output goes to stdout (or -MF file). No .o is produced. So you'd