          "text": "The user asks: is the `.d` file ultimately made from my rules in the Makefile, or from what's in the includes to a given file?\n\nThe answer: The `.d` file is generated by the compiler's preprocessor from the ACTUAL `#include` directives found in the source files — NOT from the Makefile rules. The Makefile rules only determine WHICH commands run and WHEN (i.e., the compile command includes -MMD -MP flags; make triggers compilation of a .o). The content of the .d comes from the preprocessor scanning the real include graph of that specific .cpp file.\n\nSo:\n- Makefile determines: which files get compiled (targets/rules), and that the compiler should emit .d (via flags). It also READS the .d via `-include` (making the generated deps part of the build graph).\n- The preprocessor determines: what actually goes INTO the .d — the real transitive include list of that .cpp (minus system headers).\n\nKey distinction: the .d content is ground truth from the source code, not from the hand-written T_C_H/T_CPP_H lists in the Makefile. That's exactly why it's more precise and can't go stale in the way hand-maintained lists do (it only becomes stale if you delete a header without recompiling, which -MP handles).\n\nSo the .d is derived from the includes (реальный include-граф), NOT from Makefile rules. The Makefile only orchestrates: it invokes the compiler with flags and later reads the results.\n\nLet me answer concisely and pedagogically. This is a conceptual question; no plan change needed. I should be clear and direct.\n\nAlso I can tie it back to their hand-maintained deps: the T_C_H/T_CPP_H list is \"what the Makefile author thinks the includes are\"; the .d is \"what the preprocessor actually found\". This is the fundamental difference between hand-maintained and auto-generated deps.\n\nLet me write the answer in Russian, concise.",
