ses:\n\nCase A: Header deleted, source still includes it (but you don't know / it's an intermediate header). E.g., a.hpp includes b.hpp, and b.hpp is deleted. a.hpp and .cpp unchanged. Then the .d (which lists b.hpp transitively) references a ghost. The .o is stale (compiled from a graph that had b.hpp), but nothing triggers rebuild. make errors on ghost b.hpp (without -MP).\n\nCase B: The header is removed from the include graph via another header's edit, but the directly-compiled .cpp is untouched. E.g., foo.cpp includes a.hpp; a.hpp included b.hpp. You edit a.hpp to remove `#include b.hpp` and delete b.hpp. Now: a.hpp CHANGED → make sees a.hpp newer than foo.o → recompiles → .d refreshes. So no -MP needed!\n\nHmm, so actually in most realistic cases where the include graph shrinks, SOMETHING in the chain changed (the header that dropped the include), triggering a rebuild. \n\nCase C: You delete b.hpp but NO file's text changes at all. E.g., b.hpp is deleted but nothing references it anymore — how can that be if nothing changed? If nothing references b.hpp, then the .d shouldn't list it... unless the .d is stale because it was generated when it WAS referenced. So: previously foo.cpp included b.hpp (via direct or transitive include), .d lists it. Now you delete b.hpp. But wait — if foo.cpp still (transitively) includes b.hpp, the source is broken. If it doesn't, then some file changed to remove the include → rebuild triggered.\n\nSo the ONLY truly problematic scenario is: **the .d lists a header, the header file is deleted, and no source file in the dependency chain has a newer timestamp.** For this to happen with a consistent build:\n- The header must have been included (directly or transitively) at compile time.\n- The header file is now gone.\n- Nothing that's a prereq of the .o has changed (so the compile graph, textually, either still references it — broken source — or the reference removal didn't touch any prereq... but removing a reference means editing a file that IS a prereq → newer timestamp → rebuild).\n\nHmm wait, this is subtle. Let me think again.\n\nScenario: foo.cpp includes a.hpp. a.hpp includes b.hpp. b.hpp exists. .d: `foo.o: foo.cpp a.hpp b.hpp`.\n\nNow: delete b.hpp file from disk. Nothing edited. What happens on make?\n- .d lists b.hpp. b.hpp missing → hard error (no -MP) / tolerated (with -MP).\n- But the SOURCE is now broken: a.hpp still has `#include \"b.hpp\"`. The .o was built from the old graph. The next recompile will fail with \"b.hpp: No such file\".\n- Without -MP: make fails on the ghost. With -MP: make considers foo.o fresh (a.hpp, foo.cpp unchanged), build \"succeeds\" — but the code is actually broken and WILL fail to compile next time.\n\nIn THIS scenario, the user's intuition \"расхождение между кодом и артефактами так быть не должно\" is RIGHT — there IS a real discrepancy: the source includes a header that doesn't exist. But note: this is a REAL error (broken code), not a .d bookkeeping issue. And -MP \"hides\" it until the next rebuild. Hmm. So in this scenario -MP genuinely lets a broken state slide.\n\nBut here's the thing — is this scenario realistic in a way that matters? If you delete a header that's still included, your build was already broken. The question is whether make should fail loudly NOW or at the next compile. -MP chooses \"at the next compile\". Without -MP, make fails on the ghost even if... \n\nOK let me step back and think about the ACTUAL primary scenario -MP was designed for, which is the one in the GCC docs and real-world usage:\n\nThe primary scenario: **stale .d references a deleted header because the .d wasn't regenerated.** The most common real-world case: you remove `#include \"b.hpp\"` from a.cpp, delete b.hpp. a.cpp changed → rebuild → .d regenerated WITHOUT b.hpp. No problem at all — this case self-heals! \n\nSo when does the \"ghost in .d\" actually bite in practice? It bites when the .d is NOT regenerated because the .o is NOT recompiled. For the .o not