udes bar.hpp (directly or transitively). .d lists it.\n- You delete bar.hpp (e.g., you refactored, bar.hpp's content moved into foo.hpp, and you removed the `#include \"bar.hpp\"` from... hmm).\n\nOK here's the cleanest realistic example that does NOT involve broken source:\n\n**Example:** foo.cpp has `#include \"bar.hpp\"`. bar.hpp is empty-ish. You decide bar.hpp is useless, DELETE the file, and REMOVE the `#include \"bar.hpp\"` line from foo.cpp.\n\nNow on the next make:\n- foo.cpp CHANGED (the include line removed) → foo.cpp newer than foo.o → make recompiles → preprocessor runs → new .d WITHOUT bar.hpp. Self-heals. -MP not needed.\n\nSo this case doesn't need -MP either!\n\n**Example where .d truly goes stale with healthy source:** the include is removed via a header chain edit, but the .o's own .d lists a header from the OLD chain. Hmm, but the chain edit changes a header that's a prereq → rebuild.\n\nUgh, let me think differently. When is the .d NOT regenerated while being wrong?\n\nThe .d is regenerated iff the .o is recompiled. The .o is recompiled iff some prereq in the (current) .d is newer than the .o. The current .d lists {main file + headers at last compile}. \n\nIf you delete a header that IS in the .d:\n- Without editing anything else: source still includes it (transitively) → broken source. The .o won't recompile (no newer prereq — deleted file has no mtime; other prereqs unchanged) → .d stays stale → ghost reference.\n\nIf you delete a header AND edit the .cpp to stop including it:\n- .cpp newer → recompile → .d refreshed. No problem.\n\nIf you delete a header AND edit an intermediate header to stop including it:\n- intermediate header newer → recompile → .d refreshed. No problem.\n\nSo the ONLY stale-.d-without-regeneration scenario is: **delete a header without touching anything that lists it.** And in that scenario, the source still (textually) references the deleted header → the build IS genuinely broken → -MP is deferring a real error to the next compile.\n\nHmm, so wait — is the user RIGHT that this is a code/artifact discrepancy that shouldn't be silently tolerated? In this narrow scenario, yes, there IS a real problem, and -MP tolerates it silently until the next rebuild. \n\nBUT — and here's the key counterpoint — how would you EVER get into that state cleanly? You wouldn't delete a header that's still included without noticing. The realistic flow is: you delete the header AND update the includers (in the same editing session, before running make). In that flow, the includers' mtimes change → rebuild → .d refreshes → no problem, no -MP needed.\n\nSo actually — is -MP even necessary?? Let me reconsider. Maybe the honest answer is more nuanced:\n\n-MP's value is NOT primarily for the \"delete a header\" scenario (which is either self-healing or genuinely broken). Its real value is broader: it protects against **any** ghost reference in a stale .d. And stale .d files can arise from MANY operations, not just \"delete header between builds\":\n- `git checkout` / `git pull` that removes a header (git checkout can change file contents but a removed file... hmm).\n- Renaming a file that isn't caught (the .d references old name).\n- A build tool / IDE deleting intermediates.\n- Actually, MOST importantly: **git operations**. When you switch branches or pull, files can disappear. The .d (in build/, not tracked by git, not updated by git) references a header that the new branch doesn't have. Nothing's mtime changed on the includers necessarily (git checkout sets mtime to now for changed files... actually git checkout updates mtimes of files it touches, but a deleted header just vanishes). \n\nHmm, git checkout: if the new branch has foo.cpp identical (mtime may or may not change — git sets mtime on checkout), and bar.hpp is gone in the new branch... \n\nThis is getting deep. Let me reconsider what to tell the user, being honest.\n\nThe user's core objection: \"это расхождение между кодом и артефактами — так быт