e header that dropped the include wasn't in the .d because it was a system header or... no. So (b) is basically impossible in the normal flow. Therefore:\n\n**The ONLY realistic way the .d goes stale without regeneration is (a): deleted header still referenced by source.** And that's a real bug in the code, deferred.\n\nHmm, so then what's -MP actually protecting against in practice? Let me reconsider. Maybe I'm overcomplicating.\n\nActually — I think the honest answer is: -MP is a defensive \"keep the build alive through build-description churn\" tool. Its practical value:\n\n1. **git operations**: branch switch / pull / merge can remove or rename headers in ways that don't touch the build/ dir. The .d references old names. Without -MP, make crashes on ghost headers after any such operation. With -MP, the build proceeds, and the next full/forced rebuild regenerates clean .d files. This is actually a VERY common real-world case (git checkout changing the tree under a stale build dir).\n\n2. **Manual refactoring mistakes** (case a): deferred to compile error instead of a confusing make bookkeeping error.\n\n3. **Cleanup tools** that remove files.\n\nAlso — and this is important — the user's \"это костыльно\" instinct: -MP is indeed a *pragmatic workaround for a limitation* of the make + gcc dependency mechanism. It IS a bit hacky — it adds dummy rules to paper over a stale-cache failure mode. It's not elegant. The elegant solutions are: CMake/Ninja (which regenerate dependencies properly), or the \"separate deps pass\". So the user's \"выглядит костыльно\" is actually a fair assessment! -MP IS a pragmatic band-aid over the make-based dep mechanism's cache-staleness weakness. It's the LESS-bad option in the make idiom.\n\nSo I should validate the user's intuition: yes, it's a band-aid, and yes, the discrepancy \"shouldn't happen\" in an ideal world — but in the make+gcc idiom it's the standard pragmatic choice because the alternatives are worse (hard false-failure, or