ed. -MP only matters in the narrow edge where the self-healing can't happen: a header vanished without a matching source edit — and there -MP's 'silence' is the lesser evil (build lives, compiler surfaces real errors at next compile) vs make's 'bookkeeping crash'.\"\n\nAnd validate: \"костыльно\" — fair, it IS a band-aid over make's cache-staleness. The elegant solutions come with CMake/Ninja. In the make idiom it's the standard pragmatic choice.\n\nLet me also address Q1 explicitly first.\n\nAlso I should keep the anti-sycophancy honesty: admit where the user is right (it IS a kind of discrepancy; -MP IS a band-aid; the normal flow self-heals and -MP is mostly redundant there). And correct where wrong (make won't rebuild on a missing prereq; missing ≠ newer; the loop).\n\nLet me also think about whether I should present the \"git operation\" as the main realistic trigger — I think that's actually the most honest real-world answer for why -MP exists. Let me verify: git checkout switching to a branch where bar.hpp doesn't exist:\n- build/ dir is gitignored, not touched by checkout.\n- foo.cpp might or might not change (if it also changed, it gets new mtime → rebuild → .d refreshes → -MP irrelevant).\n- If ONLY bar.hpp disappears (it existed in old branch, doesn't in new), and foo.cpp is identical → .d lists bar.hpp → ghost → without -MP: hard error. With -MP: build proceeds. THIS is the clean realistic case where the source is NOT broken (new branch's foo.cpp genuinely doesn't include bar.hpp... wait, if new branch's foo.cpp doesn't include bar.hpp, but old .d lists bar.hpp... the .d is from the old compile. New branch foo.cpp doesn't include bar.hpp → but .d (old) lists it → ghost. Next actual recompile (triggered by any future change) → .d refreshes. In the meantime, -MP keeps the build alive.)\n\nHmm wait, but in this git case, is foo.o stale? The .d lists foo.cpp (old mtime? git checkout might update it) — if git sets foo.cpp's mtime to now (checkout does update 