ewer → make recompiles foo.o → new .d without b.hpp. Self-heals. NO -MP needed.\n\nSo the \"careful refactor\" self-heals. What does NOT self-heal:\n- You delete b.hpp but forget to remove the `#include` → code is broken; make won't recompile (nothing newer) → .d ghost → without -MP: crash; with -MP: build \"lives\" until a forced rebuild reveals the compile error. In this case, is -MP \"hiding an error\"? Yes, temporarily. But the alternative (crash on bookkeeping) isn't more helpful.\n- A header disappears via git/rename tool without touching the .cpp files.\n\nHmm — actually wait. Let me reconsider whether the manual careful refactor ALWAYS self-heals. What if the include is in an intermediate header?\n\n- foo.cpp includes a.hpp. a.hpp includes b.hpp. You merge b.hpp into a.hpp: delete b.hpp, remove `#include \"b.hpp\"` from a.hpp.\n- a.hpp changed → newer → make recompiles foo.o (a.hpp is in .d) → .d refreshes. Self-heals.\n\nSo yes — any edit that removes a reference updates a tracked file's mtime → rebuild → .d refresh. The ONLY non-self-healing manual case is deleting a header that's still referenced (broken code) — where -MP defers to a compile error.\n\nSo actually — honest conclusion: in a disciplined solo workflow, -MP rarely fires. It's mostly insurance for:\n- Git/branch/tooling churn (the .d is built for a different tree state).\n- Mistakes (deleted header still referenced).\n- Any future scenario where build/ is reused with a changed source tree (which is exactly what happens when you clone/pull/switch).\n\nLet me also address their specific sub-question: \"я что руками компилирую и что-то перемещаю и потом make а внутри make уже сборка только .exe файла?\" — I think they're asking: does the scenario imply that make's build graph is somehow incomplete / make only builds the exe? No — make's full tree is fine. The issue isn't make's tree, it's the .d content being a snapshot from a previous compile. When the .d is stale, make \"knows\" wrong things — it's not that make lacks the tree, it's that the .d (which make trusts) is wrong.\n\nLet me also address: \"но имеется ввиду в общем что в нормальное выполнение makefile ворвётся какой-то другой процесс который что-то сделает с заголовочником?\" — Answer: not \"during normal execution\". The changes happen BETWEEN make invocations. The \"intruder\" is usually you (refactoring, git operations), not a rogue process. And it matters only when the change removes a header without updating a tracked source's timestamp.\n\nActually, I want to make sure I address a subtle point: the user seems to think the scenario requires \"другой процесс\" (external process). Let me clarify: no, it's usually just you, doing normal version control or file operations between builds. And the realistic value of -MP for THIS project: honestly, low for solo manual development, higher once git branches/clones come into play. I should be honest about this — -MP is cheap insurance; whether to include it is a judgment call. It's standard practice, recommended by essentially every GCC make tutorial, and it's 1 line of flags. So include it.\n\nLet me also tie to their course: this is the \"продвинутое упражнение\" territory per AGENTS.md layer 15. And CMake (layer 16) handles deps \"properly\" (it generates dependency files that... actually CMake with make uses the same .d mechanism under the hood, and with Ninja uses .ninja_deps. Ninja handles this more robustly — it regenerates deps). \n\nLet me structure my answer:\n\n**Q1:** Yes, essentially. Refine: -MP makes a specific class of make crash (ghost header from stale .d) non-fatal, trusting that the next real recompile fixes the .d. Caveat: if the underlying problem is real (deleted header still included), the next recompile will fail with a compile error — so it's not hiding that; it's deferring bookkeeping crashes while real errors surface at compile time.\n\n**Q2:** Correct the framing: it's not an external process intruding DURING a make run. It's changes BETWEEN make runs. And it's usually YOU (refactoring, git), not a rogue process. The condition for the problem: a header disappears AND no file tracked by the .d gets a newer timestamp.\n\nThen give the concrete realistic scenarios:\n1. Careful manual refactor (delete header + remove includes) → SELF-HEALS, -MP not needed. Explain why (includers' mtimes change).\n2. git operations (branch switch, pull, clone with reused build dir) → headers vanish without tracked-source changes → the realistic main case.\n3. Mistake (deleted header still referenced) → -MP defers to compile error.\n4. Tools that touch the tree (renames via tools).\n\nAnd address \"ворвётся другой процесс\": not during execution; between executions; usually you or git.\n\nAlso address: \"make уже сборка только .exe?\" — clarify make's full tree is fine; the issue is the .d snapshot, not the tree. And clarify that the problem isn't that make misses the change to .cpp — it's that no tracked source changed in the problem case.\n\nThen maybe conclude: for this project, -MP is cheap standard insurance; the realistic trigger is git/branch work and future scenarios, not daily solo editing.\n\nLet me keep it focused and not too long. The user is deep in understanding; give them a clear, honest picture.\n\nI'm in plan mode — just answer, no edits. Keep the plan reminder at end if needed. Actually the plan is still pending. Let me answer the conceptual questions thoroughly and then briefly note plan status.\n\nLet me write in Russian.\n\nDraft:\n\n**Q1.** Да, по сути так. Уточню формулировку: -MP не «глушит ошибки вообще» — он делает нефатальным один конкретный класс падения make («No rule to make target 'X.h'» от призрака в протухшем .d), рассчитывая, что следующая реальная пересборка приведёт .d в порядок. Но важная оговорка: если под несостыковкой лежит настоящая поломка (хидер удалён, а код его всё ещё включает), то -MP её не прячет — при ближайшей пересборке компилятор скажет «bar.hpp: No such f