xist, no rule → WITHOUT -MP: make FAILS on b.hpp even though a.hpp is newer and a rebuild is due!\n\nAH — THIS is the crucial point. The rebuild is DUE (a.hpp is newer), but make's rule-processing fails on the ghost b.hpp BEFORE it runs the recompile. So even in the self-healing-content case, WITHOUT -MP, make crashes on the ghost, and the recompile that would refresh the .d NEVER RUNS. WITH -MP, make passes the ghost, runs the recompile (because a.hpp is newer), and the new .d no longer lists b.hpp. Self-healed — but only because -MP let make reach the compile.\n\nSo -MP is needed precisely because the ghost reference BLOCKS the recompile that would fix it, EVEN WHEN a rebuild is legitimately due. My earlier \"self-healing without -MP\" claim (scenario 1, careful manual refactor) — let me re-examine: if you delete b.hpp AND edit foo.cpp, then foo.cpp is newer → rebuild is due → but make checks prereqs of foo.o: foo.cpp (newer — fine), b.hpp (ghost!) → WITHOUT -MP make FAILS on b.hpp before recompiling! \n\nWAIT. Is that right? Does make check all prereqs exist before rebuilding, or does it only need the target to be out of date?\n\nLet me recall make's algorithm precisely. GNU make: for target foo.o, it considers each prerequisite. For each prerequisite P:\n- If P doesn't exist: try to build P (find a rule). If no rule → ERROR \"No rule to make target 'P', needed by 'foo.o'.\"\n- If P exists but is newer than foo.o: mark that foo.o needs rebuilding.\n\nSo YES — make processes ALL prereqs, and a missing prereq with no rule is a hard error, regardless of whether a rebuild is due. So WITHOUT -MP, deleting a header that's in the .d ALWAYS crashes make, even when a rebuild is due and would fix everything.\n\nHmm wait, so then my earlier claim \"аккуратный ручной рефакторинг самолечится\" was WRONG?! Let me re-examine.\n\nScenario: delete b.hpp, remove `#include \"b.hpp\"` from foo.cpp. Next make:\n- .d lists foo.o: foo.cpp b.hpp.\n- make processes prereqs: foo.cpp exist