          "text": "The user is making a self-check konспект (study notes) and asking four questions. Let me address each carefully.\n\n**Q1: \"зачем нам mmd с компиляцией как я поняла объектника, если там и так g++/gcc который и так откомпилирует?\"**\n\nThe user asks: why do we need -MMD with compilation of the object file, if g++/gcc is already compiling anyway?\n\nThe answer: -MMD doesn't add compilation work — the compilation was going to happen anyway. -MMD adds the SIDE PRODUCT: while compiling, the preprocessor writes the .d file. Without -MMD, the compiler compiles but doesn't record dependencies. With -MMD, the same compilation ALSO produces the .d. So it's not extra work — it's \"piggybacking\" the dependency recording onto a compilation that was going to happen anyway.\n\nThe key point: you WANT the .d to be generated at the same moment as the .o, because the .d must reflect exactly what the .o was built from. And you want it generated WITHOUT an extra invocation — that's the \"cheap\" part: no separate preprocessor run. The alternative (-M/-MM) would be a SEPARATE preprocessor pass (extra command, extra cost). -MMD makes the .d free (a byproduct).\n\nSo: \"зачем mmd\" — чтобы получить .d бесплатно, не гоняя компилятор дважды. The compile happens regardless; -MMD just instructs the compiler to ALSO dump the include list during that same run.\n\n**Q2: \"что вот тут означает include что она именно делает? -include $(wildcard $(BINDIR)/*/*.d)\"**\n\nWhat does `include` do exactly?\n\nAnswer: `include` is a Makefile directive (not a shell command) that tells make: read these OTHER files as if their contents were pasted into the Makefile. In this case, the .d files are read as if their content (the `kek.o: kek.c kek.h lol.h` rules) were part of the Makefile. So make merges those dependency rules into its database.\n\n`-include` (with the dash) = ignore errors if the files don't exist (first build, no .d yet). Plain `include` would fail if a listed file is missing.\n\n`$(wildcard $(BINDIR)/*/*.d)` — the wildcard function expands to the list of existing .d files at Makefile parse time. So the line becomes `-include build/debug/common/geometry_cpp.d build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.d ...` — whatever .d files exist.\n\nSo the whole line means: \"read all existing .d files as if they were part of the Makefile; ignore any that don't exist.\"\n\nThis is what connects the generated dependencies to make's knowledge: without this include, make would never know about the .d files, and the whole -MMD mechanism would be useless (the .d files would just sit on disk ignored).\n\nImportant: this happens at MAKE PARSE TIME (when make reads the Makefile), NOT during compilation. The .d files were produced by the PREVIOUS compilation run.\n\n**Q3: \"куда именно мы подставляем -include $(wildcard $(BINDIR)/*/*.d) - при компиляции объектника в пререквизиты?\"**\n\nThe user asks where exactly we insert this line — into the prerequisites during object compilation?\n\nNo — it's NOT a prerequisite of the object rule. It's a top-level Makefile directive, placed once in the Makefile (not in any rule). It sits at the \"Makefile reading\" level: when make parses the Makefile, it reads the .d files and merges their rules into the make database. The .d rules then ADD prerequisites to the existing .o rules (make merges rules for the same target). So the dependency info flows INTO the .o rules, but the include directive itself is not part of any recipe or rule — it's a separate line in the Makefile.\n\nLet me clarify the flow:\n- The .d files contain rules like `build/debug/common/geometry_cpp.o: common/geometry.cpp common/geometry.hpp`.\n- When make reads the Makefile and hits `-include ...`, it reads those files.\n- The rules from .d are combined with the existing pattern rule for the .o (make merges prerequisites).\n- So the .o target ends up with prerequisites: from the pattern rule (.cpp) + from the .d (all transitive headers).\n\nSo the answer to Q3: No, you don't put it \"в пререквизиты при компиляции\". You put it ONCE at the top level of the Makefile (anywhere after BINDIR is defined). It's a Makefile-reading directive, not a rule or recipe line.\n\n**Q4: The user wrote their own summary (конспект) and wants me to check it.**\n\nTheir summary:\n\"добавление фантомных целей для заголовочников в .d чтобы не упало при рассинхроне такого рода например, сделали .o .d и так типа `kek.o: kek.c kek.h lol.h` это всё в рамках прогона make. далее, `lol.h` удалили и из `kek.cpp` убрали, то есть как бы честно всё. запускаем Make ещё раз, доходим до того, что надо пересобрать `kek.o` kek.o имеет пререквизитом .d, а кеш .d - старый, там всё ещё `kek.o: kek.c kek.h lol.h`, `lol.h` не существует. в обычной ситуации make падает т.к. lol.h Недостижим и no rule to make file \"lol.h\". хотя эта пересборка решила бы проблему и сделала бы .d таким `kek.o: kek.c kek.h` -MP позволяет один раз пропустить такую \"проблему\" через добавление фантомных целей, то есть\n```\nkek.o: kek.c kek.h lol.h\nkek.h:\nlol.h:\n```\nпосле обновления в .d уже нет lol.h и всё идёт нормально\nно если это реально ошибка, и lol.h удалён, но остался в include `kek.c`, то будет ошибка компилятора.\"\n\nLet me check this summary for correctness:\n\n1. \"сделали .o .d\" — yes, both produced during the same compile run.\n2. \"`kek.o: kek.c kek.h lol.h`\" — the .d rule. Correct format.\n3. \"lol.h удалили и из kek.cpp убрали, то есть как бы честно всё\" — correct scenario (header deleted + include removed). Note: they wrote kek.c and kek.cpp interchangeably — minor sloppiness but understandable; the source file is one specific file.\n4. \"запускаем Make ещё раз, доходим до того, что надо пересобрать kek.o\" — yes, because kek.c was edited (include removed) → newer → rebuild due. Correct.\n5. \"kek.o имеет пререквизитом .d\" — slight inaccuracy! kek.o does NOT have the .d as a prerequisite. kek.o's PREREQUISITES come FROM the .d (the .d lists kek.c, kek.h,