te of kek.o. Correct. Also, the .d is not a \"кэш\" in the strict sense but it's a fine way to describe it; I'll keep it.\n\nAlso, one more nuance for Q4 point: \"lol.h удалили и из kek.cpp убрали\" — in this \"честно\" scenario, the rebuild is DUE because kek.cpp changed. The user correctly identifies that. Good. And the -MP lets it proceed. Good.\n\nAlso I should note: in their phony example, they wrote `kek.h:` too — that's correct, -MP adds phony targets for ALL headers (kek.h and lol.h), not just the deleted one. Good, they got that.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider Q1 more deeply to give a satisfying answer. The user's confusion: \"-MMD\" seems to do double duty — why pair it with compilation if compilation already happens?\n\nThe essence: -MMD is a flag that CHANGES WHAT THE COMPILER DOES DURING ITS EXISTING RUN. The compile would happen anyway. -MMD tells the compiler: \"while you're preprocessing anyway, also save the list of opened include files to a .d file\". It's zero extra invocations, just a side output. Without it, the .d never gets written. The alternative (-M/-MM) is a SEPARATE command that runs the preprocessor ONLY for deps (no .o) — that WOULD be extra work (a second pass). So -MMD is the \"free\" way: deps come out of the same run.\n\nActually — let me also clarify the deeper \"why\": you need the .d to be written at the same time as the .o because the .d describes exactly what THAT .o was built from. And you need it every time the .o is rebuilt, so the deps stay fresh. Making it a byproduct guarantees it's always in sync with the .o.\n\nLet me now think about whether I should also correct anything else in their understanding of `include`. \n\n`include` in GNU make: reads the named file(s) as if they were part of the current Makefile. If an included file doesn't exist and there's a rule to make it, make tries to build it first (remake). With `-include`, missing files are silently skipped.\n\nAlso worth noting: the `$(wildcard ...)` is evaluated at parse time, so only EXISTING .d files are included. On the first build, there are none → the include line is empty → no-op. On subsequent builds, the .d from the previous run are included.\n\nAlso important nuance: the order. `include` directives are processed as make reads the Makefile, before any building. So even though .d files are created during the build, make won't see them until the NEXT invocation. This is the \"one run lag\" inherent to the mechanism — which the user's summary captures (\"кеш .d - старый\").\n\nLet me now also verify the placement answer for Q3 more concretely. Where in the Makefile? After BINDIR is defined (line 5). Common practice: near the top after variable definitions, or in a dedicated \"dependencies\" section. Since it's a Makefile-reading directive, it must come AFTER BINDIR is defined (because $(BINDIR) is expanded at parse time). So anywhere after line 5. \n\nNow — I'm in plan mode. The user is making a конспект and asking questions. This is all conceptual — no edits. I just answer. At the end, remind the plan is pending.\n\nLet me also reconsider: the user asked \"зачем нам mmd с компиляцией объектника, если там и так g++/gcc который и так откомпилирует?\" — maybe they're asking: why do we need to specify -MMD as a compile flag at all? Because the compiler doesn't write .d by default. It's a flag that must be passed. Without it, no .d. So \"зачем нам mmd\" → \"чтобы компилятор, который и так компилирует, ещё и записывал список зависимостей\". The answer to \"зачем\" is: чтобы получить .d без отдельного прогона.\n\nLet me also double check: is there any reason -MMD couldn't just be default? It's not default because writing extra files by default would surprise users; you opt in. Fine to mention briefly.\n\nLet me write the answers concisely and clearly in Russian.\n\nStructure:\n- Q1: -MMD не добавляет работы, он меняет работу существующей компиляции. Ответ.\n- Q2: include — директива чтения файлов на этапе парсинга Makefile, н