d is evaluated at parse time, and `-include` processes the expanded list. If a .d doesn't exist yet, it's simply not in the wildcard list. So first build: list is empty, `-include` is a no-op. Good.\n\nLet me also make sure about the placement: `-include` must come after `BINDIR` is defined. BINDIR is line 5. Actually, there's another subtlety: `$(BINDIR)` in the wildcard is expanded when the line is parsed. If BINDIR is defined with `:=` at line 5, then any `-include` line after line 5 can use it. Good.\n\nAlso, one more consideration: should the `-include` be placed inside the Linux ifeq block or outside? Since on Windows the wildcard would match nothing (no .d files generated by cl), `-include` with an empty list is a no-op — harmless. So it can be unconditional. But it's cleaner/clearer to place it where it's obviously about deps. Either works. I'll recommend unconditional (outside the ifeq) since it's harmless on Windows, OR inside the Linux else-branch for clarity. Hmm — actually, wait. There's a subtlety: the .d files are generated only on Linux (gcc/g++). On Windows, no .d files exist, wildcard empty, no-op. So unconditional placement works. But for conceptual clarity, some might put it in the Linux branch. I'll recommend: place it as a top-level line after the variable definitions (after line 103, after the whole ifeq block ends) — it's platform-neutral because on Windows the wildcard simply finds nothing.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — is it better inside the else (Linux) branch? The user's Makefile has the whole platform ifeq from line 11 to 103. Adding the include unconditionally after line 103 is simplest and safe. I'll go with that, noting it's harmless on Windows.\n\nLet me now also demonstrate the \"self-routing\" with a concrete example to address their confusion about \"как от одного прописывания .d окажутся где надо\":\n\nThe .d content example for t1:\n```\nbuild/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.o: t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp common/geometry.hpp common/string_utils.hpp\n```\nThe target is the FULL path of the .o. When make merges this into the rule for `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.o` (which the pattern rule also targets), it adds `common/geometry.hpp` and `common/string_utils.hpp` to that target's prerequisites. So each .d \"routes itself\" to its own .o by the target path written in its first line.\n\nSo the answer to \"как они окажутся где надо\": they don't need to be placed anywhere — make reads them all, and each file's first line already names its target. The .d self-identifies.\n\nLet me also verify the user's framing \"кроме cflags, cxxflags\" — they want to know what ELSE to add besides the flags. Answer: exactly one line: `-include $(wildcard $(BINDIR)/*/*.d)`.\n\nLet me write a clear, concrete answer with:\n1. Why wildcard expands to many files (with concrete list example).\n2. The self-routing mechanism (each .d names its own .o).\n3. The exact 2 additions (flags + the single include line) — show the full picture.\n4. Note about first-build lag and Windows no-op.\n\nI should be concise but complete. This is the culmination — they want to SEE it. Since plan mode, I present it as the plan content, not an edit.\n\nLet me show a concrete example of a .d file that would be produced, to make it tangible. For instance, after compiling t1 cpp main.o, the .d file `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.d` would contain something like:\n```\nbuild/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.o: t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp common/geometry.hpp\n```\n\nAnd the wildcard list example after a full build:\n```\nbuild/debug/common/geometry_c.d build/debug/common/geometry_cpp.d build/debug/common/geometry_c_dll.d ... build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.d ...\n```\n\nLet me also address WHY they might be confused: they might think `*/*.d` matches ONE file. No — the first `*` matches each subdirectory, so it collects from all of them.\n\nNow let me write the response.\n\nStructure:\n1. Почему много .d (wildcard explanation + c